  "raw": " the task of adventuring no longer as interesting as it once was.\nActually, in FO4 it's explained that no necromorph can actually die. They can rotting to disassembling when they reach over a millenia or so of age, but their controllers can bring them back to life—if the going gets too goddamn tough. In FO3, in fact, the putrid corpse of Abraham limbs turns back into a flesh and blood, asshole.\nGiven the other theories as to how older humans end up with such healing powers, I imagine that no necromorph ever actually dies, aside from the absorption of data into the clan. The force keeping them all alive is just too strong.\nThe same is probably true in FO3\nThe Reclamation.",
  "serialized": "the task of adventuring no longer as interesting as it once was. Actually, in FO4 it's explained that no necromorph can actually die. They can rotting to disassembling when they reach over a millenia or so of age, but their controllers can bring them back to life—if the going gets too goddamn tough. In FO3, in fact, the putrid corpse of Abraham limbs turns back into a flesh and blood, asshole. Given the other theories as to how older humans end up with such healing powers, I imagine that no necromorph ever actually dies, aside from the absorption of data into the clan. The force keeping them all alive is just too strong. The same is probably true in FO3 The Reclamation.",
